# CODEOWNERS names a responsible reviewer. It does not require anything on its
# own: branch protection decides whether the review is mandatory. A CODEOWNERS
# file with protection turned off is a comment.
#
# Last match wins, so the specific paths below override the catch-all.
#
# The extra lines are not there because those paths change more often. They are
# there because a mistake in them is not a defect that a user reports — it is
# somebody else's paid API key, somebody else's money, or a price that routes
# every generation to the wrong provider while looking correct on screen.

*                                   @Navesz

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Vendored tooling
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Vendored from the private Navesz/alicerce repository and deliberately kept
# under its original Portuguese names so it can be resynchronized with
# upstream. Renaming a file here does not break the build — it breaks the next
# sync, silently, by turning an update into a conflict nobody reads.
# Edit only to add Prumo-specific cases; upstream fixes go upstream.
/ferramental/                       @Navesz

# The checks that decide whether a change is finished, and the gate that proves
# those checks still fail on purpose. A change here can make everything else
# pass for the wrong reason, which is worse than a red build: the Herz
# `dominio-puro` rule reported "no dependency violations found" for months with
# React inside the domain.
/ferramental/verificar/             @Navesz
/ferramental/fronteiras/            @Navesz
/ferramental/portao/                @Navesz
/ferramental/segredo/               @Navesz

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Price data — wrong numbers do not crash, they lie
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Every row carries `fonte`, `coletado_em` and `metodo`, and drops out of the
# ranking after 30 days. A number changed without its source is not a smaller
# problem than a number changed wrongly — both make the router pick a route
# with confidence and the ledger report a spend that never happened.
/packages/catalog/                  @Navesz
/packages/catalog/data/             @Navesz

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Key custody — Prumo holds third-party paid credentials
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Envelope encryption (AES-256-GCM, one DEK per credential, KEK in PRUMO_KEK),
# AAD recomputed and never stored, rewrap. There is no route that reveals a
# key, not even masked: a diff that adds one is the finding, not the feature.
#
# The layer directory naming is not settled yet — PLANO.md §4 draws the server
# layers in Portuguese (`cofre/`, `provedores/`), the language rule puts code in
# English. Both spellings are claimed here so that whichever one lands, and any
# rename between them, cannot silently drop ownership of the vault.
/packages/api/src/cofre/            @Navesz
/packages/api/src/vault/            @Navesz
/packages/api/src/provedores/       @Navesz
/packages/api/src/providers/        @Navesz

# Outbound destinations are a closed list in code on purpose: there is no
# `base_url` column, because the server makes authenticated calls with the
# user's key and any endpoint coming from editable data is a credential
# exfiltration route.
/packages/api/src/http/saida/       @Navesz
/packages/api/src/http/outbound/    @Navesz

# Migrations write the shape the money lives in. `lancamento` is append-only
# with UPDATE and DELETE revoked in the GRANT; a migration that hands those
# back is a permission change disguised as SQL.
/packages/api/migrations/           @Navesz
/packages/api/src/db/               @Navesz

